ededf213204fde7c48a/d65f67e139908cbb7da561b1f8a3483203e6d3e809659dae9af22c74ece64619" "q-335748/ma-source-field/755fc6699da64d36e0918acd9ae799cb27779fc9db6aeededf213204fde7c48a/99d208b2521051360b5967239832f2efda89cbcb043a458c8469f40614ff8875"] :answer-field/correct "q-335748/ma-source-field/755fc6699da64d36e0918acd9ae799cb27779fc9db6aeededf213204fde7c48a/2352a8f34aa47c40963a4de965ba0bce7e2154b78ac50c06f3b737c0e16b0e37" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335748" :question/id #uuid "0989f91d-6c3f-5d3f-bed3-87412bdd7915" :question/math-academy-id "q-335748" :question/problem "A sensor pod of mass $16\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the pod's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after the descent began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the pod is proportional to its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k>0$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the pod’s velocity is $6\\,\\text{m/s}$ and instantaneous acceleration is $7.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nFind the constant of proportionality $k,$ and the differential equation that best models the velocity. Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the pod are weight due to gravity and drag.\n\n$\\,$ The constant of proportionality is $k=$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ The differential equation that best models the velocity is\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nIn our case, there are two forces acting on the body:\n\n- The force due to gravity, given by the weight $w$ of the body, which equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =mg \\\\  & =16g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The force due to drag (air resistance) $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity of the body (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD=-kv,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Note that, since the drag, in $N=\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ is the product of the constant $k$ and velocity, in $\\text{m/s},$ the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{N}}{\\text{m/s}}=\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2}}{\\text{m/s}}=\\text{kg/s}.\n$$\n\nSumming the forces, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=16g-kv.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the body, we have\n\n$$\n16g-kv=16\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}.\n$$\n\nWriting in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}+kv & =16g \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{k}{16}v & =g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we're given that, at a particular moment, the body's velocity is $6\\,\\text{m/s}$ and instantaneous acceleration is $7.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\nSo, substituting $v=6,g=9.8,$ and $\\frac{dv}{dt}=7.8$ into our model, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7.8+\\frac{k}{16}\\cdot 6 & =9.8 \\\\ 7.8+\\frac{3k}{8} & =9.8 \\\\ \\frac{3k}{8} & =2 \\\\ k & =\\frac{16}{3}\\,\\text{kg/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the differential equation that models the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{1}{3}v=g.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-335748/ma-source-field/575e2ec0231507f63011ee9a0ed5c5c970eec26ff400b4c57042d01e1e4f0ab0" "q-335748/ma-source-field/504517a3cb3ade921c36b873ee827281ec87bb730833848afa3109c1a6336e32" "q-335748/ma-source-field/755fc6699da64d36e0918acd9ae799cb27779fc9db6aeededf213204fde7c48a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "787c0213-46a5-50ef-8592-60c9965170f0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335748"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-336106/ma-source-field/f4ba3b7471c83c93f9340e8ec81b7a50fe8b93ff4