 :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-336106/ma-source-field/4958e5b50c226ef1ff4f162218dc5ef15c85fe855e1a0ee97c88a6a913f39d31/dc8611bde8eda73d4c60a5c96c79df36d4a174f7bc8356dca78a972abd23056c" :answer/id #uuid "d7d2992c-33d6-5911-8f6b-9bda3e200baa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the definition of a basis" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-336106/ma-source-field/4958e5b50c226ef1ff4f162218dc5ef15c85fe855e1a0ee97c88a6a913f39d31" :answer-field/id #uuid "b3682025-d096-56bd-bf59-0048dee77ced" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/choices ["q-336106/ma-source-field/4958e5b50c226ef1ff4f162218dc5ef15c85fe855e1a0ee97c88a6a913f39d31/8758c23b86c6002020022ef19dba47f13ab2ad7fc085d4265f17538f86828b05" "q-336106/ma-source-field/4958e5b50c226ef1ff4f162218dc5ef15c85fe855e1a0ee97c88a6a913f39d31/3e766754e1f76965949e819dc5824cd0034b9c80ec30ec9339435e7997ed35bf" "q-336106/ma-source-field/4958e5b50c226ef1ff4f162218dc5ef15c85fe855e1a0ee97c88a6a913f39d31/cb6fa35fda605331d612423e42e41106a03475a9e147b866fa5f45d7613e83ef" "q-336106/ma-source-field/4958e5b50c226ef1ff4f162218dc5ef15c85fe855e1a0ee97c88a6a913f39d31/dc8611bde8eda73d4c60a5c96c79df36d4a174f7bc8356dca78a972abd23056c"] :answer-field/correct "q-336106/ma-source-field/4958e5b50c226ef1ff4f162218dc5ef15c85fe855e1a0ee97c88a6a913f39d31/dc8611bde8eda73d4c60a5c96c79df36d4a174f7bc8356dca78a972abd23056c" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-336106" :question/id #uuid "accc1e17-f130-5583-b331-4d6a4ed72254" :question/math-academy-id "q-336106" :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that every solution $x(t)$ can be written in the form $x(t)=Φ(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c=[{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}]^{T}$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of solutions\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)$ can be written as $x(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)\\,c={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $x(t)=Φ(t)\\,c$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the {{field-1}} of {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first interpret line $\\text{L1}.$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the $\\text{columns}$ of $Φ,$ namely the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ These functions are the fundamental solutions of the system.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Once ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis, every solution $x(t)$ of the system can be expressed uniquely as a linear combination\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{the definition of a basis}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-336106/ma-source-field/f4ba3b7471c83c93f9340e8ec81b7a50fe8b93ff4c2e89a7cdf075b1dae781c4" "q-336106/ma-source-field/79424082b8456cf25c5a26a24478afbbad2b518e24a63f78d3c97bcfcd0ef660" "q-336106/ma-source-field/9760503da3c8361c9fb388e63ff39fce6e61628195f8dc363be0bb9805b1a386" "q-336106/ma-source-field/4958e5b50c226ef1ff4f162218dc5ef15c85fe855e1a0ee97c88a6a913f39d31"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0aea7503-74d6-5ba4-94