rect "q-337376/ma-source-field/e3be74c7b5fdd620c02e0e782a5c7686c6fdfcb7a36271f2318e501964d8c3b8/2ebc133599183e5fdb5b6248a8ea00bdd0440ff336252ee383591a8699a758ca" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337376" :question/id #uuid "a01f9794-773d-5e12-a888-6dc684788a68" :question/math-academy-id "q-337376" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=5.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{3x})}^{′}-3(u{e}^{3x}) & =5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x}-3u{e}^{3x} & =5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}} & =5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =5 \\\\ {u}^{′} & =5{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =5{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 5{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =-\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{3x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{3x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-337376/ma-source-field/65770673cd8d719b4dee3dc922de51842204857dc5ff684e2fa3cd9f617acb98" "q-337376/ma-source-field/185ae91505fc1477e8e3a03b1b4a095e521f4a3c75c36ffd5607a282ab3617bb" "q-337376/ma-source-field/e3be74c7b5fdd620c02e0e782a5c7686c6fdfcb7a36271f2318e501964d8c3b8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ac34a18-053d-5eab-8c2c-bd0f15658186"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-337376"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-337395/ma-source-field/d82f787f5e3cc83d62a9c242bc098a262da173391cd35bef0bfbb2facd7f603f/0f0bf3e5b4d49d098cc5baf660c6977edc9ec113af6f735d59f497de50943951" :answer/id #uuid "5a43a251-2319-5a92-9184-9e3033842d12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337395/ma-source-field/d82f787f5e3cc83d62a9c242bc098a262da173391cd35bef0bfbb2facd7f603f/d2e81689053b2ebae446faeee3737f8c65f277d7870bb0a42ccc7852d3d0d24d" :answer/id #uuid "cb57fa42-6e17-53a6-b42d-1f4e39f65e1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337395/ma-source-field/d82f787f5e3cc83d62a9c242bc098a262da173391cd35bef0bfbb2facd7f603f/58e66e13dc98313f1c71e73a32f04479429e770bf3852598ea62e9f4fbb454e6" :answer/id #uuid "e73f10dd-6b3a-5dd0-bdff-640976047082" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337395/ma-source-field/d82f787f5e3cc83d62a9c242bc098a262da173391cd35bef0bfbb2facd7f603f/1c87915a6c57b766bbb