f7fa8c5bf" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/choices ["q-342410/ma-source-field/964da4405d0d9996f89b0bd4da1cecb05f687bbde4a81078ae5216760ec13dd5/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" "q-342410/ma-source-field/964da4405d0d9996f89b0bd4da1cecb05f687bbde4a81078ae5216760ec13dd5/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" "q-342410/ma-source-field/964da4405d0d9996f89b0bd4da1cecb05f687bbde4a81078ae5216760ec13dd5/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937"] :answer-field/correct "q-342410/ma-source-field/964da4405d0d9996f89b0bd4da1cecb05f687bbde4a81078ae5216760ec13dd5/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "9c2710b8-4c49-5de2-8583-5f8c80fe5c91"] :question/problem "$$\nx(x-3)(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-3){y}^{′}+x(x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=-1$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=2$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=3$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-3)(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-3)(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-3){y}^{′}+x(x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x(x+1)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-3}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x(x+1)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x(x+1)=0,$ which occurs at $x=0$ and $x=-1.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x-3=0$ or where $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is undefined. These occur at $x=3$ and $x\\le 0.$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,x=0,\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)P(x)=(x+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-1,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{2}Q(x)=(x-1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-3},\n$$\n\nis undefined about $x=-1.$ So, $x=-1$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x+1},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ but the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-3},\n$$\n\nis undefined about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=3,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-3)P(x)=(x-3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=3,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}Q(x)=(x-3)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-3}=(x-3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=3.$ Therefore, $x=3$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342410/ma-source-field/ee9f3e831f316913a27fa1ebbb6ddc91caad511c547d99cbd401999a8fcc2965" "q-342410/ma-source-field/59f127ab8c99f7482daf33226c73045c0802287ccbf8f0f1f0c14db63d7fff89" "q-342410/ma-source-field/0a46c8caf236dd5bcdbd9d1788672ee5d7987ca46a274cd4d0103dd4d463c058" "q-342410/ma-source-field/964da4405d0d9996f89b0bd4da1cecb05f687bbde4a81078ae5216760ec13dd5"] :db