b9fbc3c3f65ca96eb5887fe978cb"] :answer-field/correct "q-343289/ma-source-field/84d9916550fc10b024df3eb742fe0bf6ba64cefe4d5d62dfe7bc4c434dbfdaaa/08fc3946806b1a30042891f2e1a7c0b3d141b9fbc3c3f65ca96eb5887fe978cb" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-343289" :question/id #uuid "65f6d9f7-7688-5460-b686-2b9904fe578a" :question/math-academy-id "q-343289" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(0)=-1.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(0)=-1,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=4W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =4W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =4\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =4x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{4x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=-1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{4\\cdot 0} \\\\ -1 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=-{e}^{4x}$ and $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-343289/ma-source-field/919c623672e2598151b1903520fac14d81917bfcda