c57b38239e54c0a8813/db21336f2790ae67b88375a5e536b6797e4df25c74aabb8f366ab54a7699cab4" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-343910" :question/id #uuid "68ceb45a-31c0-520d-b3cb-f81e2d91b31a" :question/math-academy-id "q-343910" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+9{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{9{\\pi }^{2}}=3\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $2$-periodic function $(L=1)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{1}=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have ${\\omega }_{3}=3\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{3}=\\omega ,$ the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x)=\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x))+x(-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)-9{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{3}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+9{\\pi }^{2}y=\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)-9{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}]+9{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)=\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6\\pi A=\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ 6\\pi B=0\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}A=-\\frac{1}{27\\pi } \\\\ B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=-\\frac{x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-343910/ma-source-field/87ff251901824b571e74be979256f8762bf3f6034d13923953296424234dcad1" "q-343910/ma-source-field/ccd9ef90f156211ecd9cf32308e2f6e8c0c1047e5c27dc57b38239e54c0a8813"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-343910"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-346720/ma-source-field/4c052bfda5dbbc552def5ee2ac251f81a8f2a14ef6e95f7680c27ecbcc78b931/1ef14df341e2f354c34ae93e3c7ddfea65840e9fda1897b5ea50de018c5615fe" :answer/id #uuid "4d40eb58-2655-5469-940e-f80c613a366a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-346720/ma-source-field/4c052bfda5dbbc552def5ee2ac251f81a8f2a14ef6e95f7680c27ecbcc78b931/b84fd199de02d074681f284eb5fe7f2e716d60e640d03dab08be8c2ffb95c521" :answer/id #uuid "9fc20b50-1f58-592c-8612-6b49865b0664" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-346720/ma-source-fi