395223f8223fd64d19be7aeada59245f1fba9beec566/b140585b3e79ed8a1f6eda2cc37a04d6e72a342ca4bb898f0b51ddecd0f585e1" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-352895" :question/id #uuid "d21173e6-c5c3-5364-8e5b-d645bc7f05bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-352895" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=1-4x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-3x})}^{′}+3(u{e}^{-3x}) & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-3u{e}^{-3x}+3u{e}^{-3x} & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{-3x}}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{-3x}} & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x} & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{3x}(1-4x) \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-3x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{-3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-352895/ma-source-field/c106593d4425620de59d0c2e43b1bf55bfecce5d3f3b72b9bb2a0533d5f6d891" "q-352895/ma-source-field/af2adab9c5e42bd7bd7ec5aebdb3bf90b6c7936a3b8ba4a2dd3b19c228b7513d" "q-352895/ma-source-field/6388b147c30844652ba7395223f8223fd64d19be7aeada59245f1fba9beec566"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ac34a18-053d-5eab-8c2c-bd0f15658186"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-352895"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-365387/ma-source-field/7dfb847bdf3bc7653de563dc3d0a54962862bf40af1b677ca91c15192c1ab47b/12e89494e4449d07c60b85624fcccbe20b1e6a8e50b5c85a7690b33c90d52b00" :answer/id #uuid "46c4364f-4fa5-52be-b64d-080c7d0e12da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t({t}^{2}-1)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-365387/ma-source-field/7dfb847bdf3bc7653de563dc3d0a54962862bf40af1b677ca91c15192c1ab47b/30e90459817c165b1c63b6207126bf30e17ca68ced82d2ed2d017525a7dffd69" :answer/id #uuid "52293ede-63c0-5c50-a1e8-a410e4858c4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-365387/ma-source-field/7dfb847bdf3bc7653de563dc3d0a54962862bf40af1b677ca91c15192c1ab47b/dae588f88bd514761e3fb9fd827644457a28699e1223aa1630d9e12efca5defa" :answer/id #uuid "7790bcee-04c3-594b-bb1b-33e4a4b043f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-365387/ma-source-field/7dfb